  "normalisations": {
    "N1": {
      "rule": "A legacy hotkey code outside 0x01-0xFE imports as unbound. Unguarded readers accept such codes: core's IniReader::ReadHex in fallout-4 and titanfall-2 reads -1, abc (0xABC), 1abc and 0x230, skyrim-special-edition reads hotkeys with strtol, and HotkeyPoller::AddHotkey stores any code. The canonical hotkey grammar holds 0x01-0xFE only.",
      "approved": null,
      "pending": "The owner's answer to open question 2 (design section 10) covers N1 only if GetAsyncKeyState never reports a code outside 0x01-0xFE, and the probe shows that 0xFF does report. Unbinding a legacy 0xFF hotkey would change that user's behaviour, so the owner chooses: (a) approve N1 as written, or (b) widen the canonical hotkey grammar to 0x01-0xFF so 0xFF carries and N1 unbinds only codes nothing can report. Until then no map uses N1, and design 4.3 defers a user holding such a code.",
      "probe": "pixi run probe-n1 (cameraunlock_tests --probe-getasynckeystate), Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200, interactive desktop, 2026-09-24: with F24 (0x87) held by SendInput, GetAsyncKeyState reported 0x87 down and reported up for 0x187, 0x287, 0x10087, -121, 0, 0xFF, 0x100 and -1, so no out-of-range code aliases a key in range. With VK 0xFF itself held, GetAsyncKeyState(0xFF) reported down. 0xFF is VK__none_, the code kbd.h gives to scan codes a layout leaves unmapped. A search of every tracked .ini and .cfg in the sibling repos found no key or toggle line holding 0xFF, 255, -1, 0 or 0x0."
    },
    "N2": {
      "rule": "A non-finite float imports as the row's default. IniReader::ReadDouble accepts nan, inf and 1e400, abzu reads its sensitivities and deadzone through it unguarded, and skyrim-special-edition's atof and std::clamp let NaN through, which gives a NaN view matrix every frame. Repos that already sanitise turn a non-finite value into their fallback on read and see no change.",
      "approved": "2026-09-24",
      "drop_rule": "NonFiniteNumber"
    }
  },
  "approved_changes": {
    "reticle": {
      "decision": 2,
      "text": "Mods do not draw their own reticles, so the canonical format and the preference set have no reticle toggle and no ShowReticle concept. A migration drops a legacy reticle toggle hotkey, ShowReticle and reticle colour, and the conversion documents the loss.",
      "approved": "2026-09-24",
      "drop_rule": "Reticle"
    },
    "pose_shaping": {
      "decision": 3,
      "text": "The tracker owns pose shaping, so sensitivity multipliers, deadzones, response curves and user-facing axis inversion leave every mod and the canonical format has no rows for them. A value a player set away from the shipped default is dropped. A shipped default that is not identity (abzu InvertRoll=true, [Position] InvertX=true, a roll multiplier other than 1) was correcting the mod's axis conversion, so the conversion folds it into the mod's boundary code and default behaviour does not change.",
      "approved": "2026-09-24",
      "drop_rule": "PoseShaping"
    },
    "follows_default": {
      "decision": 4,
      "text": "A feature shipped disabled pending verification (CollisionEnabled, for example) takes its intended default in the conversion and follows core's default from then on, instead of keeping the value a legacy file holds.",
      "approved": "2026-09-24",
      "drop_rule": "FollowsDefault"
    }
  },
  "hotkey_exceptions": {},
  "allow_legacy_symbols": {}
}
